  "body": "Today, every browser still requests `/favicon.ico` by default, and this has been going on forever.\r\n\r\nI wonder if we could mimic the automatic network request whenever a `<script type=\"module\">` is present on the current page, once per domain / session, pointing at `/mjs.json`.\r\n\r\n<sup>there is also an [explicit alternative](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3056#issuecomment-330828512) with same benefits and less \"magic\"</sup>\r\n\r\nThe goal of this file would be to provide a valid JSON with just one role: define how to resolve modules that don't use relative or absolute URLs.\r\n\r\n### Example\r\n\r\nThe following page contains just one script.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<script type=\"module\">\r\nimport hyper from \"hyperhtml\";\r\naddEventListener('load', () => {\r\n  hyper(document.body)\r\n    `<h1>Welcome in ${location.hostname}</h1>`;\r\n}, {once: true});\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSince there is at least one `<script type=\"module\">`, the browser would look once for the file `/mjs.json`, which in this case will contain the following JSON:\r\n```js\r\n{\r\n  \"hyperhtml\": \"https://unpkg.com/hyperhtml@latest/min.mjs\",\r\n  \"lodash\": \"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lodash.js/4.17.4/lodash.min.js\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter reading such valid JSON file, the browser will resolve the import from either `hyperhtml` or `lodash` using the provided absolute URL.\r\n\r\n### Pros\r\n\r\n  * **security**: the website owner is the only one responsible for such file, no CORS should ever be enabled\r\n  * **statically valid**: it is never possible to change this file, or the URL pointer, at runtime\r\n  * **polyfillable**: this file can be used to instrument loaders/bundlers and somehow polytfill the described behavior/resolution for modules.\r\n  * **smart cache**: browsers might pre-load modules ahead of time when the network is not busy, and/or cache them when possible to have same resolution across different sites\r\n  * **performance**: HTTP2 and CDNs finally get their role back instead of being nullified by bundles\r\n  * **portability**: every module can point at a relative/absolute path like it is already for ES6 modules on browsers.\r\n\r\nSpecially last point ensures JS code can be shared and ported across different environments that uses different bundlers or different sources based on `package.json`.\r\n\r\n### Cons\r\n\r\n  * one extra, non blocking, call before starting the ES2015 modules logic\r\n\r\nConsidering the compactness of the single-role JSON file, on average also smaller than most 404 pages when it comes to `/favicon.ico` requests, the cons shouldn't be considered a stopper.\r\n\r\nBeing ES2015 modules also relatively new on the Web, this shouldn't have side effects or backward compatibility issues, but it's not difficult to place a `/mjs.json` file with content `{}` to avoid 404s.\r\n\r\nLooking forward for any sort of question/outcome.",
